    ],
    "derivation": "A stationary ship at the origin running INTERCEPT_TARGET (desired range 100, never reached) against a ship at (3000, 0, 8000) flying along -x at 100 m/s (forward (-1, 0, 0), constant-velocity motion: x(k) = 3000 - 100 * k / 120). setTarget starts the curve at tick 0 with four copies of the tick-0 position at ticks 0, -60, -120, -180 (startTrackingCurve 5425-5442; times in seconds are tick / 120 on the integer clock of contract item 8). updateTrackingCurve runs at the start of each update and takes a sample when ticks[0] + 60 <= now (5448): at ticks 60, 120, 180, shifting positions[0] into [1] and the new position into [0] (5450-5465). calculateTrackingCurve fits a quadratic through samples 1 to 3 only (5469-5489), so the tick-60 sample is invisible: after tick 60 samples 1 to 3 are still all the tick-0 position, and the aim point is exactly (3000, 0, 8000) at ticks 0, 90 and 119. After the tick-120 sample, samples 1 to 3 are the target at ticks 60, 0 and -60 (positions (2950, 0, 8000), (3000, 0, 8000), (3000, 0, 8000)); the quadratic through (s = 0, P(60)), (-0.5 s, P0), (-1 s, P0) in s = now - times[1] is P0 + v * (s + 0.5)(s + 1) with v = -100 m/s, evaluated at s = 0.5 s: P0 + 1.5 v = x 2850 at tick 120; at tick 121 s = 0.5 + 1/120 and the lead is v * (1 + 1/120)(1.5 + 1/120). After the tick-180 sample the three samples are the target at ticks 120, 60 and 0, collinear, so the fit is exact: the aim point at 180 is the target's true position P0 + 1.5 v = 2850 (it was 150 m ahead of the target at tick 120). calculateTargetPosition evaluates at [UNIVERSE getTime] (5421). If Oolite sampled one tick later (61, 121, 181) the aim point at tick 120 would still be (3000, 0, 8000), 150 m behind the 2850 expected here, and at 180 the first fit would not yet be exact; ticks 120 and 180 therefore separate the two rules, and ticks 59, 60 and 61 (a base-dependent edge) are deliberately not checked. Tolerance 1e-6 m (positions are doubles).",
